-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Each edit names its starting revision, identified by a revision ID. Concurrent edits—submissions derived from the same revision—produce competing complete revisions. A coordinator resolves conflicts by selecting or manually constructing a merged revision; competing submissions remain persisted as-is. This approach preserves the worker's submitted visit as an indivisible unit and requires no maintained merge rules, at the cost of treating even unrelated field changes as conflicts requiring review.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A visit's revision graph forms a tree: the first submission has no parent; subsequent submissions cite their starting revision. If two submissions share the same parent revision and both are accepted, the visit enters an unresolved state pending coordinator review.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Tablet-sync tablets track the base revision ID returned by the server at last successful sync and show field workers pending unresolved revisions before allowing new edits. Review-export presents revision comparison and excludes unresolved visits from its export set until all conflicts are resolved.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+## Submission Envelope for Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+A submission carries:
+- `visit_id` (UUID): worker-assigned, stable across edits
+- `base_revision_id` (string, nullable): server revision ID from the last retrieved state, or null for the first submission
+- `author_id` (string): authenticated worker identity
+- `idempotency_key` (UUID): unique per attempted submission; retries use the same key
+- `fields` (object): the complete visit data (observation date, observer list, species estimates, notes)
+- `submitted_at` (timestamp): wall-clock time on the tablet
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+When accepted, the server returns:
+- `revision_id` (string): stable identifier for this submission; used as `base_revision_id` in subsequent edits
+- `is_resolved` (boolean): true if this is the current accepted state; false if the visit now has competing revisions pending review
+- `competing_revision_ids` (list, empty if `is_resolved` is true): revision IDs of other accepted submissions with the same `base_revision_id`
+
+## Current-State Retrieval
+
+When a caller (tablet or coordinator) retrieves a visit by ID:
+- Return the complete fields of the current accepted revision (the first submission if no coordinator action has occurred; the coordinator-selected revision if review work was resolved)
+- Include `is_resolved` and `competing_revision_ids` so the caller can decide whether to block new edits or show pending conflicts
+- Include the full history: all submission envelopes (minus raw payload) in order of server receipt, showing author, timestamp, and revision ID
+
+## Coordinator Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator POST to resolve review work:
+- Names the visit ID and a chosen revision ID (from among the competing set or a manually constructed ID)
+- Optionally includes a new merged `fields` object if the chosen revision differs from any original submission
+- The system records the choice, names the contributing submission IDs, retains the coordinator's identity, and marks the visit `is_resolved = true`
+- If a new merged revision was constructed, it is stored as a synthetic submission with author `coordinator`, linking its parent to the original competing revisions
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Area | Decision | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: competing edits create review work for coordinators | See [decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md) | Field-aware three-way merge (detailed in decision doc) |
+| Revision identifier | Server-assigned strings, returned to tablet | Stable across sync and clear reference point for both tablet and coordinator. | Client-assigned UUIDs (reduces server state but complicates tablet logic). |
+| History representation | Full submission history with author, timestamp, revision ID visible to both tablets and coordinators | Enables tablets to show workers what changed and when, and coordinators to inspect the audit trail. | Stripped history (smaller payloads but less explainability). |
